          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression is a direct, conventional avowal presenting Pierre's love for Hélène as true. Its formulaic and embarrassed delivery may affect sincerity or conviction, but not its clear truth-conditional avowal force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"да и я люблю ее\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"In this engagement setting, Pierre's avowal weakly participates in ratifying his socially presumed role as Hélène's future husband. Nevertheless, the words themselves do not explicitly promise marriage, fidelity, or any other future action, so the surrounding betrothal cannot simply be transferred onto their primary force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"что нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The utterance is not represented as a reflex-like discharge of affect. Pierre pauses, considers what convention requires, remembers the expected phrase, and then says it deliberately.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual core force is needed beyond the direct avowal and its weak contextual role in ratifying the presumed engagement. Its awkward conformity to social convention concerns how the avowal is performed rather than constituting a separate mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Hélène's father has publicly treated Pierre and Hélène as engaged and blessed them, they are left alone. Hélène kisses Pierre; Pierre then recalls the conventional words expected on such an occasion and tells her, “Je vous aime,” feeling ashamed at how poorly the words sound.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"mixed\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Pierre's words weakly ratify his assumed position as Hélène's fiancé, but they contain no explicit undertaking to marry or perform another future act. Hélène gives no verbal response to this particular utterance in the supplied passage. The family has already treated the pair as engaged, and the passage reports that they marry a month and a half later, providing practical uptake without changing the target's primarily avowing force. Evidence of affection is mixed: Pierre internally says that he loves her and is strongly attracted to her, but his dread, social pressure, hesitation, and formulaic delivery leave the quality and voluntariness of that affection uncertain.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace. The classification relies only on the supplied text rather than additional remembered plot information.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage provides rich immediate context but cannot conclusively establish Pierre's sincerity, the depth or durability of his love, or Hélène's interpretation of this exact sentence. Her lack of a verbal reply here should not be taken as evidence that the wider work contains no further response or relevant context.\"}"
